The U.S. Plastics Recycling Economy — Thomas, Kneifel and Butry (@mat-plastics-recovery: 0, is #reference, #part-of @mat-sources) { r_4FMXC9V02YNV4DFSCCV0CVR8JN
Douglas S. Thomas, Joshua D. Kneifel and David T. Butry. *The U.S. Plastics Recycling Economy: Current State, Challenges, and Opportunities*. NIST AMS 100-64, October 2024. [Official publication](https://www.nist.gov/publications/us-plastics-recycling-economy-current-state-challenges-and-opportunities), [complete 117-page PDF](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ams/NIST.AMS.100-64.pdf), DOI 10.6028/NIST.AMS.100-64.

Read the complete report. Chapter 3 introduces mechanical and chemical routes; chapters 4–5 relate collection, markets and infrastructure to actual throughput. Follow its cited primary process studies for mechanism, yield, feed composition and product-quality evidence. The report complements polymer physics and degradation readings rather than replacing them.

Official metadata, contents and introductory discussion were inspected on 30 October 2026; all process studies and tables were not independently reproduced. Direct download returned 403, while browser access worked. Historical prices, regulations and facility inventories retain their dates and geographic scope.

Official free NIST publication; preserve separately credited material and the linked NIST use conditions. No market database or media is copied into the repository.

**Return:** [Materials-engineering sources — reading and primary evidence](../Sources.lingua#r_51JAH6DB4WV2HZAB69G21ZHSMK).
